diff --git a/docs/priority-filter-20261030/README.md b/docs/priority-filter-20261030/README.md
new file mode 100644
index 0000000..e515ff5
--- /dev/null
+++ b/docs/priority-filter-20261030/README.md
@@ -0,0 +1,123 @@
+# Samsung priority UID filter
+
+Current extension: [automatic root-app selection](../auto-root-apps-20261031/README.md).
+
+Adaptation of [upstream PR #158](https://github.com/inforcqb/susfs4ksu-for-lkm/pull/158),
+reviewed at `d55749105beaac03776d95241f4400a68292f831`, onto Samsung source
+`961fb6a0585c4ad503f9a9e1fe107058d3595c92`.
+
+## Behavior and improvements
+
+The standard `0x60030` command accepts a comma-separated package list. A successful
+command replaces the entire list; an empty list clears it. Selected app IDs answer
+`ESRCH` to priority-by-UID operations from untrusted/umounted app callers. Root,
+system/shell identities in every Android user, trusted KernelSU profiles, and the
+selected app itself retain the kernel's normal behavior. Ordinary PID/process-group
+operations also retain their original handlers.
+
+Compared with the proposal:
+
+- All four native and four AArch32 wrappers have separate original pointers and
+  span the complete callback lifetime, including sleeping originals and return tails.
+  The existing verified restoration and Tasks-RCU drain remain unchanged.
+- Installation is all-or-none, including compat coverage on a compat kernel. A
+  failed optional layer rolls back and refuses the command; core SusFS stays usable.
+- The set path uses the existing root caller's credentials. It never edits a private
+  SELinux blob, assumes a fixed root domain, or manipulates Samsung credential refs.
+  A denied lookup returns an error and preserves the previous list.
+- Names, directory ownership, namespace and input termination are validated before
+  publication. Immutable bitmaps are replaced under a mutex and retired after RCU;
+  readers do not allocate, look up paths or modify credentials.
+- The caller boundary excludes system IDs in every Android user but includes SDK
+  sandboxes (20000-29999) and app-zygote isolated children (90000-98999), as well as
+  ordinary apps and isolated processes. AOSP defines these ranges in
+  [Process.java](https://github.com/aosp-mirror/platform_frameworks_base/blob/main/core/java/android/os/Process.java).
+  Limiting callers to 10000-19999/99000-99999 would leave a query route around
+  filtering. The existing KernelSU umount/trusted-profile gate still applies.
+- `ioprio_set` uses the running kernel's actual `ioprio_check_cap` before returning
+  `ESRCH`. Otherwise an invalid priority would expose a different error for hidden
+  and genuinely absent UIDs. The BZJA binary inlines this check in its syscall;
+  the 19-instruction validation prefix matches the callable helper after register
+  and branch-label normalization.
+- Root can read readiness/count/generation from the read-only `priority_filter`
+  module parameter. There is no new invented `CONFIG_*` feature line.
+
+The standalone module defaults to no configured targets unless automatic boot
+parameters are supplied. The app now enables automatic root-app protection and
+refreshes installed-manager extras after unlock; see the extension above. The
+four priority entries remain installed while the optional layer is initialized.
+
+## ABI and control client
+
+The payload has `char app_strings[65535]`, one alignment byte, then `int err` at
+offset 65536; total size is 65540. Old commands and layouts are unchanged.
+
+`rmgu-priority-ctl` is a freestanding ARM64 PIE, with no libc, interpreter or
+dynamic relocations. The APK packages the same executable as
+`librmgususfs_priority.so`; the test artifact also provides the standalone name.
+
+```
+rmgu-priority-ctl status
+rmgu-priority-ctl set com.example.one,com.example.two
+rmgu-priority-ctl query 10123
+rmgu-priority-ctl probe 19998 10123
+rmgu-priority-ctl clear
+```
+
+Run these as root. `probe` requires the target app to be running and observable as
+root before it can give a meaningful result. It creates its own mount namespace,
+makes its mounts private, clears supplementary groups and retained privileges,
+then drops to the requested ordinary caller UID. This keeps KernelSU's setuid
+unmount action inside that private namespace. It only reads priorities and sends
+an intentionally invalid `ioprio_set`, never a valid scheduling change. It also
+checks self visibility and correct invalid-input errors. The output is JSON;
+0 means verified, 1 means the expected filtering/result was not observed, and 2
+means invalid arguments or an inconclusive/blocked probe.
+
+`query` is a read-only observation under the caller's existing UID and SELinux
+context, suitable for comparison from a real app shell and from root. It makes
+no filtering verdict without a known running-target baseline. The automated
+probe tests UID/namespace behavior; it is not a complete app/SELinux sandbox test.
+`configured` counts unique app IDs, including shared-UID deduplication.
+
+## Limits and phone validation
+
+This is UID filtering on four syscalls, not complete package/process hiding.
+Packages sharing an app UID are inseparable. App IDs apply across Android users;
+configuration resolves `/data/data/<package>` in user zero. Packages existing
+only in another Android user need a separate future resolver. Set the list after
+first unlock, when credential-encrypted app-data directories can be resolved.
+
+The module retains numbers, not package observers or inode/mount references.
+Reapply the list after installing, uninstalling or reinstalling selected apps:
+UID changes can leave a stale rule, and UID reuse can otherwise filter a different
+package. Failed updates preserve the last successful list; clear is explicit.
+Rules are boot-local and must be reapplied after a full reboot.
+
+No phone test is claimed here. Install the signed test APK, **fully reboot**, root
+and activate SusFS, then check `ready=1` before configuring a harmless running app.
+Use an ordinary caller app ID distinct from the target. A synthetic caller such
+as 19998 should be checked against the phone's actual assigned IDs first; the
+probe changes only its own process IDs. Test each backend/firmware actually used.
+Do not attempt to replace a live module with `rmmod`.
+
+## Reproduction and evidence
+
+```
+python3 tools/run_host_suite.py --output build/priority-host
+python3 tools/build_review.py --kdir /opt/ddk/kdir/android16-6.12 \
+  --clang-bin /opt/ddk/clang/clang-r536225/bin \
+  --rust-bin /opt/ddk/rust/rust-1.82.0/bin \
+  --evidence-dir build/priority-module --omit-module-btf --jobs 2
+python3 tools/build_priority_client.py \
+  --clang-bin /opt/ddk/clang/clang-r536225/bin --output build/rmgu-priority-ctl
+python3 tools/check_priority_artifact.py --candidate kernel/susfs_guard_lkm.ko \
+  --baseline /path/to/961fb6-module.ko --kdir /opt/ddk/kdir/android16-6.12 \
+  --clang-bin /opt/ddk/clang/clang-r536225/bin --output build/priority-artifact
+```
+
+Host models exercise production C, including fault injection and negative controls.
+They are not execution of an ARM64 kernel. `check_priority_bzja.py` additionally
+checks recovered symbols/CFI and the actual I/O validation prefix of the pinned
+BZJA image. Empty import-version tables still prevent a complete CRC/ABI claim.
+Records in this directory identify the compiler/build and all validation results.
